<p>China’s National People’s Congress (NPC) has removed nine military representatives from its legislature just ahead of the country’s annual “Two Sessions” political meetings, according to multiple reports. Authorities have not publicly explained the removals. They come as the People’s Liberation Army (PLA) faces a broader anti-corruption drive tied to President Xi Jinping’s push to tighten discipline and control over the armed forces. The shake-up has also drawn attention abroad: the Financial Times notes that those dropped include an officer linked to a unit it describes as critical in any potential Taiwan contingency, a reminder that personnel moves can ripple into regional security debates.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ITG4rAHrdhMJwzjNEIdz9YZre8vyJVGHM1GCa5AiDv5y5UNUBfPRabXIsYUOEmWMywwq0YUeUAVutS8BPz7UC93KI0CEQw-UcopNHcl05-XDF-EiOkyV4VspDnKzLAxsyKqYgYuOIjvPe-ZXdGBSNWQsQIOm1uJmgsF1M5VyPpWk4VEz' alt='China’s legislature removed nine military deputies ahead of the annual political meetings.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Who was affected: Firstpost said five of the nine military representatives removed were full generals, highlighting how senior the officials were.</li><li>Why it matters: Firstpost cited the International Institute for Strategic Studies (IISS) warning about command deficiencies in China’s armed forces—context that can heighten concern when leadership changes pile up during a corruption drive.</li><li>What the forum signals: The Financial Times described the NPC as a “rubber-stamp” parliament, suggesting that removal is less about parliamentary accountability and more a visible marker of internal political or disciplinary action.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>BBC: The BBC said officials gave no public reason for the removals, underscoring how little explanation has been offered. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c6270z0dr6no'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Semafor: Semafor described the move as a widening of Xi Jinping’s purge campaign just ahead of the “Two Sessions” meetings. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/02/26/2026/china-purges-more-military-officials-ahead-of-two-sessions'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Financial Times: The Financial Times focused on the national-security angle, reporting that the purge included a commander from a unit it described as critical to a potential Taiwan invasion. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/602bd27e-d28f-4deb-8748-b939fd818b37'>Financial Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/china-removes-nine-military-officials-ahead-of-annual-political-meeting-ws-e-13984132.html'>China removes nine military officials ahead of annual political meeting</a> - firstpost.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/02/26/2026/china-purges-more-military-officials-ahead-of-two-sessions'>China purges more military officials ahead of &#x27;two sessions&#x27;</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c6270z0dr6no'>China removes nine military officials ahead of key political meeting</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/602bd27e-d28f-4deb-8748-b939fd818b37'>China sacks 9 senior military officers as Xi widens crackdown</a> - ft.com</li></ul>